# Managing User Sessions

Registration and login work. There is still something absurd about the result: the server has no idea, one request later, that the login happened. Every private page would need the password again.

The cause is the statelessness from the cookies reading. HTTP is **stateless**: the server treats every request as arriving from a brand-new stranger, because as far as HTTP is concerned it is. It does not remember that you logged in one second ago.

A **session** is the fix, and the useful image is a temporary visitor badge. You show your credentials once at the desk, you are handed a badge, and after that you show the badge.

We will build this by hand to see how it works, in `auth-demo/04-sessions/`. **In production, use a library**: `express-session` or Passport, which is later in this lesson. Sessions are security-critical and there is no prize for a homemade one.

## The Shape of It

Remember from the cookies reading that a server can attach a cookie to a response, and the browser then sends that cookie back with every later request to the same site. Sessions ride on exactly that:

1. The user logs in successfully.
2. The server invents a **session ID**: unique, and impossible to guess.
3. The server remembers, on its side, which user that ID belongs to.
4. The server sends the ID to the browser as a **cookie**.
5. Every later request carries that cookie automatically, so the server can look the ID up and know who is asking.

The password crosses the network once. After that, the badge.

## Handing Out the Badge

```{walkthrough}
title: The server side of a session
step: Somewhere to keep them
file: auth-demo/04-sessions/server.js
region: sessions
@"const sessions": An ordinary object, mapping each session ID to what the server remembers about that user. Keep the word "memory" in mind; it comes back at the end of this reading.

step: On a successful login
file: auth-demo/04-sessions/server.js
region: login
The login endpoint from stage 3, unchanged until the moment the password matches. Then, instead of only saying so, it starts a session.
@"crypto.randomUUID": The ID has to be unguessable. The video started with `'xyz'` as a placeholder and immediately called it inappropriate: anyone who can guess a valid session ID *is* that user, without a password. `crypto.randomUUID()`, from Node's built-in `crypto` module (imported at the top of the file), produces a random identifier nobody can predict.
@"sessions[sessionId] = {": The server's half of the arrangement: this ID belongs to this email.
@"Set-Cookie": The browser's half. The `Set-Cookie` header rides along with the `200`, so the badge arrives together with the "you are in".
```

The three cookie attributes on that `Set-Cookie` line are the ones from the cookies reading, and each is doing a job here:

- **`HttpOnly`** keeps JavaScript from reading the cookie, so a script injected into your page cannot steal the session.
- **`SameSite=Strict`** stops the browser sending it on requests originating from other sites.
- **`Max-Age=3600`** expires the badge after an hour. The unit is seconds.

**And notice what is *not* in the cookie**: no email, no name, nothing about the user. Only an opaque ID. Everything real stays on the server, which is the whole point of the pattern.

## Reading the Cookie Back

The `Cookie` header arrives as one string, so it has to be parsed. Again, a library would do this; doing it once shows what the library is for:

```{walkthrough}
title: parseCookies
file: auth-demo/04-sessions/server.js
region: parse-cookies
@"req.headers.cookie": Where the browser's cookies show up. A first-time visitor sends none at all, and then this header is not there, which is why the function returns the empty object rather than assuming.
@"split(';')": Cookies arrive as one string, separated by semicolons: `theme=dark; sessionId=abc123`.
@"[name, ...rest]": Each piece is `name=value`, so split at `=`. The `...rest` is a rest element: the first part becomes `name` and *everything else* lands in the `rest` array. That matters on the next annotated line.
@"name.trim": The pieces are separated by `; `, semicolon and a space, so without a `trim()` the space would become part of the next cookie's name.
@"rest.join('=')": A value can itself contain `=` characters. If the cookie was `data=xy=z`, splitting produced `['data', 'xy', 'z']`, and gluing the tail back together with `=` restores the whole value, `xy=z`. Joining an array of one item gives back that item on its own, so the ordinary case works too.
```

## The `/me` Endpoint

Now a route that answers "who am I?":

```{walkthrough}
title: GET /me
file: auth-demo/04-sessions/server.js
region: me
@"cookies.sessionId": Parse the cookies, then look for the one that matters here.
@"sessionId && sessions[": Both halves of the check matter. A request may carry no `sessionId` cookie at all, or carry one the server does not recognize, and neither should be treated as logged in.
@"JSON.stringify": A known badge answers with what the server remembered at login, as JSON, with the content type saying so.
@"writeHead(401": Anything else is `401 Unauthorized`, the same status a failed login gets.
```

## Using It From the Pages

Two client-side changes put the session to work:

```{walkthrough}
title: The pages
step: login.html goes home on success
file: auth-demo/04-sessions/static/login.html
region: script
The same login script as before, except for what happens after the `fetch`.
@"response.ok": True for any 2xx status, so it distinguishes success from the `401` without reading the number.
@"window.location.href": On success, navigate back to the home page instead of printing a message. The failure branch still shows the server's text.

step: index.html asks who it is talking to
file: auth-demo/04-sessions/static/index.html
region: user-info
A welcome message that starts hidden. The `style="display:none"` means a visitor who is not logged in never sees it.

step: checkSession
file: auth-demo/04-sessions/static/index.html
region: script
@"async function checkSession": The function has to be `async` because of the `await`s inside it.
@"fetch('/me')": No headers, no credentials, nothing about who is asking: the browser attaches the session cookie by itself. That silent tag-along is the property that makes cookies right for this job.
@"style.display": On a `200`, fill in the email and reveal the welcome block by switching its `display` to `block`.
@"checkSession();": Small enough to overlook, and load-bearing: defining an `async` function does nothing until something calls it. In the video, the missing call was a real moment of "why is nothing happening". Forgetting it produces no error message at all.
```

## Sessions Live in Memory

Restart the server while logged in, then refresh. You are logged out, and the browser still has its cookie.

`const sessions = {}` is an ordinary variable, so it lives in the server's memory and dies with the process, exactly as in the hit-counter reading. The badge is still in the visitor's pocket; the desk has thrown away its records, and an ID that matches nothing is treated like a made-up one.

For development that is merely annoying. In production it means nobody stays logged in through a deployment, which is why real session stores keep this in a database or a dedicated store such as Redis. It is the same lesson as Lesson 2, arriving again.

## Parsing and Lookup, Running

The two server-side moves, runnable here:

```{node autorun}
function parseCookies(request) {
  const list = {};
  const cookieHeader = request.headers.cookie;
  if (!cookieHeader) { return list; }

  const cookies = cookieHeader.split(';');
  for (const cookie of cookies) {
    let [name, ...rest] = cookie.split('=');

    if (name) { name = name.trim(); }
    else { continue; }
    const value = rest.join('=').trim();
    if (!value) { continue; }
    list[name] = value;
  }
  return list;
}

const sessions = {
  'e9b1c7a4-1f2d-4c3b-8a9e-2d4f6b8c0a1e': { email: 'soney@umich.edu' }
};

const cookies = parseCookies({
  headers: { cookie: 'theme=dark; sessionId=e9b1c7a4-1f2d-4c3b-8a9e-2d4f6b8c0a1e' }
});

console.log(cookies);
console.log('this request is from ' + sessions[cookies.sessionId].email);

// A visitor who has never logged in.
console.log(parseCookies({ headers: {} }));
```

Try changing the `sessionId` in the fake header to something the `sessions` object does not contain and see what breaks; that is the case the `/me` endpoint's `if` is guarding.

## Running It Yourself

From the `auth-demo` folder (stop the previous stage with `Ctrl+C` first):

```
$ node 04-sessions/server.js
Listening on port 3000
```

1. Register, then log in. The login page bounces you back to the home page, and the home page greets you by email: that greeting is `checkSession` hearing back from `/me`.
2. Open the developer tools (F12), find the cookies for `localhost` (Application > Cookies in Chrome), and there is `sessionId`, a long random UUID. That string is the badge.
3. Visit `http://localhost:3000/me` directly. Logged in, you get your session as JSON; in a private window, `Unauthorized`.
4. Now restart the server (`Ctrl+C`, run it again) and refresh the page. The cookie is still in the browser, and you are logged out anyway: the `sessions` object was reborn empty.

Logging in works and persists across pages. What we cannot do yet is leave. Logging out is the next reading.

## Exercises

1. Complete `parseCookies`.

    ```{node}
    function parseCookies(request) {
      const list = {};
      const cookieHeader = request.headers.cookie;

      if (!cookieHeader) {
        return list;
      }

      for (const cookie of cookieHeader.split(';')) {
        // TODO: split the cookie at '=', trim the name, re-join the rest
        // as the value and trim it, then store the pair in list
        // (skip a piece with no name or no value)
      }

      return list;
    }

    console.log(parseCookies({ headers: { cookie: 'theme=dark; sessionId=abc123' } }));
    ```
    ```+solution
    function parseCookies(request) {
      const list = {};
      const cookieHeader = request.headers.cookie;

      if (!cookieHeader) {
        return list;
      }

      for (const cookie of cookieHeader.split(';')) {
        let [name, ...rest] = cookie.split('=');

        if (name) { name = name.trim(); }
        else { continue; }

        const value = rest.join('=').trim();
        if (!value) { continue; }

        list[name] = value;
      }

      return list;
    }

    console.log(parseCookies({ headers: { cookie: 'theme=dark; sessionId=abc123' } }));
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(parseCookies({ headers: { cookie: 'theme=dark; sessionId=abc123' } })).should('equal', { theme: 'dark', sessionId: 'abc123' }).run('Split each piece at `=` and store the trimmed name and value', 'Parsed both cookies');
    wrap(parseCookies({ headers: { cookie: 'sessionId=abc123' } }).sessionId).should('equal', 'abc123').run('It should work for a single cookie too', 'Parsed one cookie');
    wrap(parseCookies({ headers: { cookie: 'data=a=b=c' } }).data).should('equal', 'a=b=c').run('A value can contain `=`, so re-join everything after the first split piece. `rest.join(\'=\')` does this', 'Kept an equals sign inside the value');
    wrap(parseCookies({ headers: {} })).should('equal', {}).run('A visitor with no cookies sends no header, so return an empty object', 'Handled a missing header');
    ```

2. Complete the `/me` handler.

    ```{node}
    const sessions = {
      'abc123': { email: 'soney@umich.edu' }
    };

    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleMe(cookies, response) {
      // TODO: if the cookies contain a sessionId that the server knows,
      // answer 200 with Content-Type application/json and the session as JSON.
      // Otherwise answer 401 with the body 'Not logged in'.
    }

    const loggedIn = makeFakeResponse();
    handleMe({ sessionId: 'abc123' }, loggedIn);

    console.log(loggedIn.statusCode + ' ' + loggedIn.body);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const sessions = {
      'abc123': { email: 'soney@umich.edu' }
    };

    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleMe(cookies, response) {
      const sessionId = cookies.sessionId;

      if (sessionId && sessions[sessionId]) {
        response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
        response.end(JSON.stringify(sessions[sessionId]));
      } else {
        response.writeHead(401, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        response.end('Not logged in');
      }
    }

    const loggedIn = makeFakeResponse();
    handleMe({ sessionId: 'abc123' }, loggedIn);

    console.log(loggedIn.statusCode + ' ' + loggedIn.body);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(loggedIn.statusCode).should('equal', 200).run('A known session should answer `200`', 'Recognized the session');
    wrap(loggedIn.body).should('equal', '{"email":"soney@umich.edu"}').run('Send the session object as JSON, using `JSON.stringify`', 'Sent the session as JSON');
    wrap(loggedIn.headers && loggedIn.headers['Content-Type']).should('equal', 'application/json').run('Announce the body as `application/json`', 'Set the content type');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleMe({ sessionId: 'not-a-real-id' }, r); return r.statusCode + '|' + r.body; })()).should('equal', '401|Not logged in').run('A session ID the server does not know should answer `401` with `Not logged in`', 'Refused an unknown session');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleMe({}, r); return r.statusCode + '|' + r.body; })()).should('equal', '401|Not logged in').run('A request with no `sessionId` cookie at all should be refused the same way', 'Refused a request with no cookie');
    ```

3. What are sessions for?

    ```{mcq}
    What problem does a session solve?
    correct: Correct! It is a visitor badge: the server records who a session ID belongs to, the browser carries the ID, and the server can recognize the user on every later request without asking for the password again.
    wrong: Think about what the server cannot do on its own between two requests from the same person.
    [x] They let the server remember a user's identity across separate requests
    [ ] They encrypt password data on the user's hard drive
    [ ] They let JavaScript rewrite the page without contacting the server
    [ ] They make the browser delete its cookies after login
    ```

4. You are logged in, you restart the server, you refresh, and you are logged out, even though the browser still has its cookie. Why?

    ```{mcq}
    What was lost in the restart?
    correct: Correct! `sessions` is an ordinary variable in the server's memory, so restarting empties it. The cookie is still valid-looking, and the ID in it now matches nothing. This is why real applications keep sessions in a database or a session store.
    wrong: The cookie survived, so the missing half is on the other side of the connection.
    [x] The `sessions` object lived in the server's memory, which is emptied when the process restarts
    [ ] The browser notices the restart and deletes its cookies
    [ ] `crypto.randomUUID()` changes how the cookie is decoded after a reconnection
    [ ] `SameSite=Strict` treats a server restart as a cross-site attack
    ```

